From 88080e1d606019694ee59bdf6a9956f2e3f4a3ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Andrei V. Lepikhov" <lepihov@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:53:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH v0 08/10] Move the rarely used parts of NumericAggState out of
 line

An int128 fast-mode sum(numeric)/avg(numeric) state never touches the
digit-array accumulators or the NaN/Inf counters, yet carried them
inline: 160 bytes, which AllocSet rounds up to a 256-byte chunk per
group.  Move them into NumericAggStateExt, allocated on first use
(promotion, the first finite input of a sumX2 state, or the first
special value), so that a fast-mode group costs one 64-byte chunk.

With hash aggregation over many groups the transition state is the
per-group memory, so this roughly halves HashAgg memory for
sum(numeric)/avg(numeric): 168 MB to 80 MB for 500k groups with one
aggregate and 304 MB to 120 MB with two. More groups fit in memory before
spilling as well: at work_mem = 4MB the two-aggregate query goes from
161 batches to 33.  The per-row cost of a transition does not change, since
the fields it touches stay inline.

Variance-family states get two chunks (64 + 128 bytes) instead of one
256-byte chunk.  That is still less memory (232 MB to 208 MB for 500k groups)
at the same speed.  Carving the out-of-line part from the same chunk for those
states was tried and rejected: the combined request lands in the 256-byte size
class, costing more memory.

A has_specials flag in the inline part, set once a NaN or infinity has been
counted, lets readers of the special-value counters skip the out-of-line chunk.
Without it every final function dereferenced it just to learn that the counters
were zero, a cache miss per group that made sample variance over single-row
groups slower than before.

Only a state's owner allocates its out-of-line part.  Combine, serialize and
the final functions take any other state as it is: one without the part has put
nothing into the digit-array accumulators and counted no special value, so it
stands for zero sums and counters.

NOTE:
aggtransspace for sum(numeric) and avg(numeric) deliberately stays at 128,
although a fast-mode state is now 64 bytes. Planner's cost model might cause
unusual plan flips that causes suboptimal plans in some situations.

Add a regression test with NaN and infinities confined to some workers of
a parallel partial aggregation, plus a group with only NULL inputs, which
exercises the out-of-line part through serialization and combining.
